02:57:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The short answer is that **GPS is a one-way street**. The satellites do not communicate back and forth with your phone; they don't even know you exist. Because the satellites are only broadcasting and never receiving data from users, it doesn't matter if there is one person using GPS or ten billion people using it. The satellites do the exact same amount of work. Here is a breakdown of how this works: ### The Radio Station Analogy Think of a GPS satellite like an FM radio tower. A radio tower broadcasts music in all directions. It takes the exact same amount of energy to broadcast to one car radio as it does to a million car radios. The radio tower doesn't know who is listening, and it doesn't have to "handle" the listeners. GPS satellites work the exact same way, but instead of broadcasting music, they broadcast two pieces of information: 1. **Who they are** (their exact location in space). 2. **What time it is** (down to the nanosecond, using highly accurate atomic clocks). ### How Your Phone Does All the Work Your smartphone, car navigation system, or smartwatch is just a passive listener. It has a GPS receiver antenna that catches these signals. Here is how your device figures out where you are: 1. Your phone receives a signal from a satellite. 2. It looks at the time the satellite sent the signal, and compares it to the time your phone received the signal. 3. Because radio waves travel at a constant speed (the speed of light), your phone can calculate exactly how far away that satellite is based on the time delay. (For example, if the signal took 0.06 seconds to arrive, your phone knows the satellite is exactly 11,100 miles away). 4. Your phone repeats this process with at least **four different satellites**. 5. By drawing invisible spheres of distance around those four satellites, your phone finds the one single point where all those spheres intersect. That intersection point is your exact location on Earth. (This mathematical process is called *trilateration*). ### Why a "Handful" of Satellites is Enough There are currently about 31 active GPS satellites orbiting the Earth. They are arranged in very specific, high-altitude orbits (about 12,500 miles up). They are spaced out so perfectly that from almost any point on the surface of the Earth, at any given time of day, there is a direct line of sight to at least 6 to 10 satellites. Since your phone only needs 4 to figure out your 3D location (latitude, longitude, and altitude), this "handful" of satellites is more than enough to cover the entire globe. ### A Note on Privacy Because GPS is a one-way broadcast, **the GPS system cannot track you**. When people talk about being "tracked via GPS," what they actually mean is that their phone used the passive GPS signals to figure out its own location, and then an *app* on the phone (like Google Maps, Uber, or a fitness tracker) sent that location data to the internet via a cellular or Wi-Fi network.
