utely* sure the system works. * **New Civilian Signals:** The satellites themselves have evolved. Modern satellites like the GPS Block III satellites are more complex because they broadcast dedicated civilian signals (L1C, L2C, L5) that didn't exist when the system was purely military. They are literally doing more work with more complex broadcasting, supporting the needs of civilians. * **International Considerations:** The US has to keep GPS top-tier because Europe (Galileo), China (BeiDou), and Russia (GLONASS) have their own. These competing systems, along with the reliance of allies, forces us to push the envelope in performance and reliability. * **Historical Turning Point:** Remember Selective Availability? That was intentionally introduced to degrade accuracy for civilian users. It was turned off in 2000. That’s when the shift from "military first" to "global utility" really cemented itself. Ultimately, the satellites don't feel the weight of global dependence. However, the US Space Force and the entire system architecture works *far* harder to keep the system flawless today than they did in the 1980s.
